<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conan IV, Duke of Brittany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1138–<a href="/years/1171">1171</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conan IV was duke of Brittany from 1156 to 1166 and earl of Richmond in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Under pressure from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a>, he gave up power in 1166 and betrothed his daughter and heiress Constance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s son Geoffrey, bringing Brittany into the Angevin sphere. He kept only the county of Guingamp and died in 1171.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conan-iv-duke-of-brittany.jpg" alt="Conan IV, Duke of Brittany" width="320" height="324" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dom Morice · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conan_IV_de_Bretagne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Conan, called the Young, was the son of Bertha, Duchess of Brittany, and her first husband, Alan, 1st Earl of Richmond. From his father he inherited the earldom of Richmond, which made him a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of both the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and the King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. When his mother died early in 1156 his stepfather, Odo II, Viscount of Porhoët, refused to give up power, perhaps in league with Conan's uncle Hoel, Count of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>. Within the year Conan captured and imprisoned Odo and took his inheritance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Brittany had long been largely independent, with its own language and culture, and most of it was held by local lords. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> claimed to be its overlord and at first ruled through proxies, backing Conan, who had strong English ties. After Henry's brother Geoffrey, who had deposed Hoel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>, died in 1158, Conan briefly held the city, but Henry opposed him, and at Michaelmas 1158 Conan surrendered Nantes and its lands to the king at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a>. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-vii-of-france">Louis VII of France</a> did not intervene.</p><p>In 1160 Conan married Henry's cousin Margaret, sister of the Scottish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-lion">William the Lion</a>; their daughter was Constance. Facing revolts by his nobles, possibly encouraged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Conan asked Henry for help, and Henry demanded Constance's betrothal to his son Geoffrey. Henry seized lands on the Norman border in 1164 and invaded Brittany in 1166 to punish the barons, then forced Conan to abdicate in favour of Constance, an unusual arrangement since Conan might still have had sons. Henry held Brittany as her guardian, though he never claimed the ducal title himself.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Conan died on 18 or 20 February 1171. He and Constance were the only members of the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/penthievre">Penthièvre</a> to rule Brittany. A son named William appears to have been alive towards 1200. Breton historians have usually remembered Conan as a weak ruler unable to defend his duchy against the Angevin king, a view the historian Eric Borgnis-Desbordes has recently qualified. He appears in Jean-François Ducis's tragedy of 1791 and in novels by Sharon Kay Penman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conan IV of Brittany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Brittany’s position between the Angevin and Capetian worlds drew it into their wars, and Henry II of England forced Duke <b>Conan IV</b> to surrender power in 1166.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
